Jane is the human-facing predecessor to the internal agent layer. Jane's output should be normalized into an Intent Envelope. Internal agents consume the envelope rather than parsing raw conversation independently.

{  "intent_uid": "INT-...",  "tenant_uid": "TEN-...",  "user_uid": "USR-...",  "studio": "DATA|ML|AGENTIC",  "goal": "...",  "constraints": ["..."],  "data_refs": ["..."],  "desired_outputs": ["..."],  "risk_class": "normal|high|critical",  "user_role": "...",  "conversation_summary": "...",  "language": "en-IN"}

The Metaphor layer uses the intent envelope only to choose the appropriate vocabulary/context. It must not change the user's technical intent.

POST /api/v1/compiler/jobsContent-Type: application/json

Example:

{  "request_id": "REQ-20260814-0001",  "user_intent": "Prepare the machine temperature and production data for predictive maintenance.",  "input_assets": [    {      "asset_id": "ASSET-001",      "name": "machine_data.zip",      "uri": "object://incoming/machine_data.zip"    },    {      "asset_id": "ASSET-002",      "name": "production.xlsx",      "uri": "object://incoming/production.xlsx"    }  ],  "domain": "industrial_manufacturing",  "requested_output": {    "format": "aiconnex_unified"  }}

Example:

{  "plan_version": "1.0",  "confidence": 0.94,  "operations": [    {      "id": "OP01",      "type": "UNZIP",      "input": "A001"    },    {      "id": "OP02",      "type": "APPEND",      "inputs": [        "A001_FILE01",        "A001_FILE02",        "A001_FILE03"      ]    },    {      "id": "OP03",      "type": "JOIN",      "left": "OP02",      "right": "A002",      "keys": [        "machine_id",        "timestamp"      ],      "join_type": "LEFT"    }  ],  "warnings": [],  "requires_confirmation": false}

The Compiler validates this before execution.

Example:

{  "dataset_id": "ACX-DS-000001",  "dataset_uri": "object://unified/ACX-DS-000001.parquet",  "format": "parquet",  "schema_version": "1.0",  "schema": [    {      "name": "machine_id",      "dtype": "string",      "semantic_type": "ENTITY_ID"    },    {      "name": "timestamp",      "dtype": "timestamp[us, UTC]",      "semantic_type": "TIMESTAMP"    },    {      "name": "temperature",      "dtype": "float64",      "semantic_type": "MEASUREMENT",      "unit": "degC"    }  ],  "row_count": 1845023,  "quality": {    "null_rate": 0.012,    "duplicate_rate": 0.001,    "schema_valid": true  },  "provenance_uri": "object://reports/ACX-DS-000001-lineage.json",  "recipe_uri": "object://reports/ACX-DS-000001-recipe.json"}

This becomes the input to Data Profiler.

Jane captures the human's intent and negotiation about the plan. The output should be a structured intent, not merely conversation text.

{  "intent_uid": "INT-...",  "user_goal": "Monitor compressor health and alert maintenance",  "site_uid": "PLANT-01",  "asset_scope": ["COMP-02"],  "desired_outcome": "early_warning",  "autonomy_requested": "RECOMMEND",  "notification_channels": ["dashboard","email"],  "constraints": {    "business_hours": true,    "maintenance_team": "ROTATING_EQUIPMENT"  }}

Agent Builder converts this intent into a signed Agent SPEC. The Main Agent never treats arbitrary later chat text as permission to expand its capabilities.

Jane's output should become an explicit Visualization Intent rather than remaining only natural language.

{  "intent_uid": "INT-...",  "type": "industrial_monitoring",  "site_scope": ["PLANT-01"],  "asset_scope": ["LINE-A"],  "requested_views": ["live_state","anomalies","predictions"],  "time_range": "live",  "alert_visibility": true,  "explainability": true,  "user_role": "industrial_operator",  "permissions": ["read_telemetry","view_predictions"]}

If scope is ambiguous, Jane asks for clarification instead of silently selecting every plant or asset.

Jane is the conversational intent layer. The output becomes a structured intent contract for Agentic Studio. The knowledge foundation does not reinterpret the user's intent freely; it receives the intent UID, desired outcome, constraints and requested autonomy and uses those to select permitted evidence and capabilities.

{  "intent_uid": "INT-...",  "goal": "Monitor compressor health and alert maintenance",  "constraints": {    "site_uid": "PLANT-01",    "asset_family": "compressor",    "time_window": "24h"  },  "requested_autonomy": "INFORM",  "user_principal": "operator"}

Agent Builder then compiles the intent into the signed Agent SPEC. The Runtime supplies that SPEC to this stage as the governing contract.

Tools are executable capabilities, not general-purpose access. Each tool has a typed schema, scope, side-effect classification, identity mapping, timeout, rate limit and idempotency policy.

{  "tool_uid": "create_work_order",  "version": "2.0",  "side_effect": "WRITE",  "capability": "MAINTENANCE",  "input_schema": "WORKORDER-V3",  "allowed_asset_scope": "site:PLANT-01",  "requires_approval": true,  "idempotency": "required",  "timeout_seconds": 20}

OWASP identifies excessive agency as a consequence of excessive functionality, permissions or autonomy. AI-ConneX addresses this by keeping tools explicitly registered and passing calls through policy and the Runtime/Tool Gateway. citeturn0search2turn0search8

The six components converge into an Evidence + Capability Context. This is what the Agent Runtime consumes before asking a model to propose a decision.

{  "intent": {...},  "asset_context": {...},  "telemetry": [...],  "graph_facts": [...],  "documents": [...],  "model_refs": [...],  "available_tools": [...],  "policy": {...},  "provenance": [...]}

The Evidence Pack is bounded: it should contain only what the SPEC permits and what the current execution needs.
